---
title: Strigidae
---
# Strigidae

Strigidae is the larger owl family, around two hundred species of nocturnal
predator with a round facial disc and, in many species, erectile tufts on the
crown. They take prey after dark by stillness and a silent approach rather than
by speed, and a few species remain abroad in the gloom of a winter afternoon.
Eggs are laid in a tree cavity or a bare scrape, and incubation starts with the
first egg, so the eldest chick is far the largest.
